# GPS navigation satellite, modelled from published dimensions rather than
# traced from any CAD data.  This file is the spacecraft bus;
# gps-bus.obj, gps-panels.obj and gps-antenna.obj together are the
# satellite, sharing one coordinate frame, so the demo stands all three
# with the same transform.
#
#   bus                box, 2.5 x 2.1 x 1.8 m
#   solar wings        two panels a side, 13.1 m tip to tip
#   antenna array      12 L-band elements on a 1.7 x 1.7 m deck, facing Earth
#
# Sources: eoportal.org GPS spacecraft table (Block IIF 2.5 x 2.1 x 1.8 m,
# 13.1 m span including the solar arrays; Block III 1.8 x 2.5 x 3.4 m,
# 15 m span), Lockheed Martin GPS III/IIIF data sheet (dual solar array,
# nadir pointing, specialised L-band antenna arrays).
#
# Coordinates are bpp scene units, not metres: 1 unit = 637.1 km, so the
# 13.1 m wingspan comes out at 0.524 units against the Earth's radius of
# 10 units -- the model is drawn about 25,000 times life size, because a
# real satellite would be a few tens of micrometres wide on this stage.
# (metres x 0.04 = units)
#
# Axes: +x along track (direction of travel), +y across track, +z up;
# the antenna array faces -z, which the demo points at the Earth's
# centre.  Faces are triangles; each corner carries its face's own normal,
# so the shading stays flat, and its own texture coordinate: each face
# spans 0..1 of the texture once (planar projection), so the demo's
# textures tile per face.
